% ============================================================================
% noun-type — three named noun types and what each actually is
% (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Nouns fall into several overlapping categories: a common noun is the
% generic name of an item in a class or group, a collective noun names a
% group or collection of people or things, and an abstract noun names
% something that cannot be perceived by the senses. Recall is a binding
% query:
%
%     ? noun_type(common_noun, $D)
%       % generic_name_of_an_item_in_a_class_or_group
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `noun_type(type, definition)` carrying the source citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns
% the definition WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a noun type that is not one of these six. The query also
% runs BACKWARD — bind a definition and recall the noun type it names:
%
%     ? noun_type($T, cannot_be_perceived_by_the_senses)
%       % abstract_noun
%
% The six noun types, as a little truth table:
%
%     type              definition                                    quote (verbatim)
%     ----------------  --------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     common_noun        generic_name_of_an_item_in_a_class_or_group   "A common noun is the generic name of an item in a class or group."
%     collective_noun     denotes_a_group_or_collection_of_people_or_things  "A collective noun denotes a group or collection of people or things."
%     abstract_noun       cannot_be_perceived_by_the_senses             "An abstract noun is something that cannot be perceived by the senses."
%     concrete_noun       perceived_by_the_senses_physical_or_tangible  "A concrete noun is something that is perceived by the senses, something physical or tangible."
%     countable_noun      can_be_counted                                "Countable nouns can be counted, even if the resulting number would be extraordinarily high"
%     uncountable_noun    impossible_to_count                           "Uncountable nouns, or mass nouns, are nouns that are impossible to count"
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "Nouns: Definition and
% Examples" article, which gives a clean, single-sentence definition for
% each of common, proper, concrete, abstract, collective, singular,
% plural, countable, uncountable, and gerund nouns. WebFetch-verified
% before writing (three times total across this file's original three
% rows and this extension's three added rows). `trust consensus` —
% Grammarly is a reputable, widely-cited grammar-content company, the
% same tier this stdlib already reserves for other non-.gov language
% sources and the same source family already cited by
% `sentence-type.adj`/`part-of-speech.adj`/`possessive-noun.adj`/
% `superlative-adjective-rule.adj`. Picked using the mandatory
% full-tree-grep-before-scoping discipline —
% `grep -rilE "\bnoun_type\b|\bcommon_noun\b|\bcollective_noun\b|
% \babstract_noun\b|\bconcrete_noun\b|\bcountable_noun\b|
% \buncountable_noun\b" code/specs/data/adj-facts-stdlib/` found ZERO hits
% (beyond this file's own existing rows), confirming a completely fresh
% topic before this file was written. `concrete_noun` deliberately pairs
% with the already-shipped `abstract_noun` (perceived by the senses vs.
% not), and `countable_noun`/`uncountable_noun` form their own natural
% pair, extending this table's original three rows to six.
%
% A noun-related concept that is NOT one of these six is deliberately
% NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a
% definition. This includes THREE candidates from the SAME source page
% that were deliberately excluded for bundling two distinct facts into
% one sentence rather than stating a single clean fact the way each
% tabled row does: `proper_noun` ("A proper noun is a specific name of a
% person, place, or thing AND is always capitalized" — bundles the
% naming function with a separate capitalization rule), `singular_noun`
% ("...refers to one person, place, thing, or idea AND requires a
% singular verb" — bundles what it refers to with a grammatical
% agreement rule), and `gerund` ("...a verb form that ends in -ing AND
% functions as a noun in a sentence" — bundles a morphological fact with
% a syntactic-function fact); `plural_noun` bundles the same way as
% `singular_noun` and is excluded for the same reason. `possessive_noun`
% is also NOT a row here — a real noun type the same page mentions and
% describes functionally, but for which it gives no single formal
% one-sentence definition the way it does for the six tabled here (and a
% distinct concept from the already-shipped `possessive-noun.adj`, whose
% `possessive_noun(word, category)` table classifies possessive-FORM
% examples like `dog`/`bottles`/`geese`, not the general definition of
% "what is a possessive noun" that this file would need). (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table noun_type {
    columns type, definition

    row (common_noun, generic_name_of_an_item_in_a_class_or_group)
    row (collective_noun, denotes_a_group_or_collection_of_people_or_things)
    row (abstract_noun, cannot_be_perceived_by_the_senses)
    row (concrete_noun, perceived_by_the_senses_physical_or_tangible)
    row (countable_noun, can_be_counted)
    row (uncountable_noun, impossible_to_count)

    source "A common noun is the generic name of an item in a class or group."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/nouns/"
    trust consensus
}
